ly \\(L_{3}\\), whose slope is \\(-2\\) (sunny).  \n  Thus the third line would be sunny, contradicting the requirement of exactly two sunny lines.\n\n* **Case \\(\\{L_{1},L_{3}\\}\\):**  \n  Covered points: \\(A,F\\) and \\(D,C\\).  \n  Uncovered points: \\(B(1,2)\\) and \\(E(3,1)\\).  \n  The line through \\(B\\) and \\(E\\) is \\(L_{2}\\), a sunny line, again a contradiction.\n\n* **Case \\(\\{L_{2},L_{3}\\}\\):**  \n  Covered points: \\(B,E\\) and \\(D,C\\).  \n  Uncovered points: \\(A(1,1)\\) and \\(F(2,2)\\).  \n  The line through \\(A\\) and \\(F\\) is \\(L_{1}\\), sunny, yielding the same contradiction.\n\nIn every possible selection of two sunny lines, the two points left uncovered are collinear on a sunny line. Hence it is impossible to have a third, non‑sunny line that covers exactly those two points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\nNo arrangement of three distinct lines can satisfy the covering condition while having exactly two sunny lines. Therefore for \\(n=3\\) the integer \\(k=2\\) is **not attainable**.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, there is no configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines.}}\n\\]"
