            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nFor \\(n=3\\) there is **no** configuration of three distinct lines that covers all required points while exactly two of the lines are sunny. Hence the integer \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **List the required points**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P=\\{A(1,1),\\;B(1,2),\\;C(2,1),\\;D(1,3),\\;E(3,1),\\;F(2,2)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Classify every line determined by two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).**  \n   Computing all slopes shows that the only *sunny* lines (slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)) are  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{1}:AF\\;(y=x),\\qquad   \n   L_{2}:BE\\;(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52),\\qquad   \n   L_{3}:DC\\;(y=-2x+5).\n   \\]\n\n   Every other line through two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is parallel to the\n   \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence it is *non‑sunny*.\n\n3. **Show that each point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on at most one sunny line.**  \n   Direct inspection of the three sunny lines gives  \n\n   \\[\n   A,F\\in L_{1},\\; B,E\\in L_{2},\\; C,D\\in L_{3},\n   \\]\n   and no point appears on two different sunny lines. Consequently a sunny\n   line can contain **at most two** of the required points, and if it contains\n   one point it automatically contains a second one (the partner listed above).\n\n4. **Count how many points the two sunny lines can cover.**  \n   Because the two sunny lines are distinct, they cannot share a point\n   (see step 3). Hence they together cover exactly four distinct points of\n   \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n5. **Identify the non‑sunny lines that can cover the remaining points.**  \n   Non‑sunny lines are horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)), or diagonal\n   (\\(x+y=c\\)). Among the six points, the only such lines that contain **three**\n   points are  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1\\;(A,B,D),\\qquad y=1\\;(A,C,E),\\qquad x+y=4\\;(D,E,F).\n   \\]\n\n   All other non‑sunny lines contain at most two points.\n\n6. **Check the three possible choices of two sunny lines.**  \n   The possible pairs are \\(\\{L_{1},L_{2}\\},\\{L_{1},L_{3}\\},\\{L_{2},L_{3}\\}\\).\n\n   *For \\(\\{L_{1},L_{2}\\}\\):* covered points are \\(A,F\\) and \\(B,E\\); the\n   uncovered points are \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).  The line through \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) is\n   \\(L_{3}\\), which is sunny – contradicting the requirement that the third\n   line be non‑sunny.\n\n   *For \\(\\{L_{1},L_{3}\\}\\):* uncovered points are \\(B\\) and \\(E\\); the line\n   through them is \\(L_{2}\\), sunny – contradiction.\n\n   *For \\(\\{L_{2},L_{3}\\}\\):* uncovered points are \\(A\\) and \\(F\\); the line\n   through them is \\(L_{1}\\), sunny – contradiction.\n\n   In every case the two points left uncovered lie on a sunny line, so the\n   third line cannot be non‑sunny.\n\n7. **Exclude configurations where a sunny line covers only one point.**  \n   If a sunny line contained a single required point, the other point of that\n   line would have to be outside \\(\\mathcal P\\).  However, for each point of\n   \\(\\mathcal P\\) the unique sunny line through it is one of \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\),\n   which already contains a second point of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  Hence any sunny\n   line meeting \\(\\mathcal P\\) necessarily contains two points, and step 4\n   applies.\n\n8. **Conclusion:**  \n   No selection of exactly two sunny lines can leave the remaining points on a\n   single non‑sunny line, and a non‑sunny line cannot cover more than three\n   points.  Therefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does not\n   exist for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, }k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
