            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\cup\\{n\\}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\},           & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\;}\n\\]\n\nIn words: all non‑negative integers \\(k\\le n\\) are possible, except that when \\(n\\) is even the value \\(k=n\\) cannot be realised.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   N_n=|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **A counting bound.**  \n   *Any sunny line meets the triangle \\(\\operatorname{conv}(T_n)\\) in at most*\n   \\[\n   s_n=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\displaystyle\\frac{n}{2}, & n\\ \\text{even},\\\\[2mm]\n   \\displaystyle\\frac{n+1}{2}, & n\\ \\text{odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   (Proof: a sunny line has a rational slope \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) with\n   \\(p,q\\neq0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\); the lattice points on it are spaced\n   by the vector \\((q,p)\\).  Inside the triangle the maximal number of\n   such points is obtained for the slope \\(1\\), giving exactly the\n   values in (1).)\n\n3. **Necessary condition.**  \n   If a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines,\n   then by (1) the total number of covered points is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\,s_n+(n-k)\\,n .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Requiring (2) to be at least \\(N_n\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1, & n\\ \\text{even},\\\\[2mm]\n   n,   & n\\ \\text{odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible; for odd \\(n\\)\n   no restriction on \\(k\\) follows from (3).\n\n4. **Construction for all \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   We prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for every integer\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\)\n   with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Base \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=-2x+5,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;2y=-x+5\n   \\]\n\n   cover the six points of \\(T_3\\); by omitting any of them we obtain\n   configurations with \\(k=0,1,2\\).  Thus the statement holds for\n   \\(n=3\\).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume the statement true for \\(n-1\\).  Take the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V:\\;x=1,\n   \\]\n\n   which is non‑sunny and contains exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n   Removing these points leaves the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{n-1}'=\\{(a-1,b)\\mid (a,b)\\in T_n,\\ a\\ge2\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which is a translate of \\(T_{n-1}\\).  By the induction hypothesis,\n   for any integer \\(k'\\) with \\(0\\le k'\\le n-2\\) (if \\(n-1\\) is even)\n   or \\(0\\le k'\\le n-1\\) (if \\(n-1\\) is odd) we can cover\n   \\(T_{n-1}'\\) with \\(n-1\\) distinct lines having exactly \\(k'\\) sunny\n   ones.  Adding the non‑sunny line \\(V\\) yields a configuration for\n   \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k=k'\\) sunny lines.  Because the right‑hand\n   bound in the induction hypothesis equals \\(n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even\n   and equals \\(n-2\\) when \\(n\\) is odd, we obtain **all** values\n   \\(0\