for every sunny line }L .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWhen \\(n\\) is odd, \\(s_n=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) is odd and the line\n\\(y=x\\) shows that a sunny line can contain an odd number of points.\nWhen \\(n\\) is even, \\(s_n=\\frac{n}{2}\\) is even and any sunny line\ncontains an even number of points (otherwise two points of the line\nwould have the same abscissa or ordinate, forcing the line to be\nvertical or horizontal). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  Impossibility of even values of \\(k\\;(k\\ge 2)\\)\n\nAssume that a configuration with exactly \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines exists.\nLet the sunny lines be \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) and the remaining\n\\(n-k\\) lines be non‑sunny.\nPut  \n\n\\[\nU:=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k} (S_i\\cap\\mathcal T_n),\\qquad\nV:=\\mathcal T_n\\setminus U .\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 2.1,\n\\(|U|\\le k\\,s_n\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|V|=|\\mathcal T_n|-|U|\n      \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2-k\\,s_n .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nEvery non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\)\n(because the longest vertical, horizontal or diagonal line in the\ntriangle has length \\(n\\)).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n|V|\\le (n-k)\\,n .\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3.1) and (3.2) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le k\\,s_n+(n-k)n .\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nInsert the two possible values of \\(s_n\\).\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd*, \\(s_n=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) and (3.3) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le k\\frac{n+1}{2}+(n-k)n\n\\Longleftrightarrow\nk\\ge n .\n\\]\n\nThus for odd \\(n\\) a configuration with an even \\(k\\ge 2\\) would force\n\\(k=n\\), but \\(n\\) is odd – a contradiction.\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*, \\(s_n=\\frac{n}{2}\\) and (3.3) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le k\\frac{n}{2}+(n-k)n\n\\Longleftrightarrow\nk\\ge n-1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is assumed even, the only possible value would be\n\\(k=n\\); however \\(n\\) is even and Lemma 2.2 shows that the total\nnumber of points covered by the sunny lines,\n\\(k\\,s_n