ogether contain all six points.\n\n*Construction for \\(k=1\\).*  \nChoose the sunny line \\(y=x\\) (covers \\((1,1),(2,2)\\)) together with the two non‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) (covers \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\)) and \\(y=1\\) (covers \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\)).  Every point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of these three lines, and exactly one of them is sunny.\n\n*Construction for \\(k=3\\).*  \nTake the three sunny lines listed above; they are distinct and together contain all six points.  Thus \\(k=3\\) is attainable.\n\n*Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).*  \nAssume that exactly two of the three lines are sunny.  Because each sunny line contains precisely the two points shown in the table, the two sunny lines together cover **four** distinct points of \\(T_3\\).  The two points that remain uncovered are necessarily a pair of the form \\(\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\}\\), \\(\\{(2,1),(1,3)\\}\\) or \\(\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\}\\).  In each case the unique line through the uncovered pair is one of the three sunny lines already used, contradicting the requirement that the third line be non‑sunny.  Hence no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\n\nConsequently for \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A bound for sunny lines when \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad \n\\text{If }L\\text{ is sunny, then }|L\\cap T_n|\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the points of \\(L\\cap T_n\\) (if any) as \\((x_i,y_i)\\) with increasing\n\\(x\\)-coordinates.  Because the points are collinear, the differences\n\\((\\Delta x,\\Delta y)=(x_{i+1}-x_i,\\;y_{i+1}-y_i)\\) are constant; denote them\n\\((u,v)\\) with integers \\(u\\neq0,\\;v\\neq0\\) and \\(u\\neq -v\\) (the latter excludes\nslope \\(-1\\)).  The sums satisfy  \n\n\\[\n(x_i+y_i) = (x_1+y_1)+ (i-1)(u+v).\n\\]\n\nAll these sums are \\(\\le n+1\\); hence the common difference \\(d:=u+v\\) must be\n\\(\\le0\\).  If \\(|d|\\ge2\\) then already the first three points would give a sum\ndecrease of at least \\(2\\) each step, forcing the third point to have either\n\\(x\\le0\\) or \\(y\\le0\\), contradicting positivity.  Therefore \\(|d|\\le1\\).\n\n*Case \\(d=0\\).*  Then \\(u=-v\\) and the slope is \\(-1\\), which is forbidden for a\nsunny line.\n\n*Case \\(|d|=1\\).*  Up to symmetry we may assume \\(d= -1\\); then \\(v = -u-1\\).\nBecause \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) are non‑zero, the only possibilities are\n\\((u,v)=(1,-2)\\) or \\((u,v)=(-2,1)\\).  In either case the step changes the sum by\n\\(-1\\); starting from a point with sum at most \\(n+1\\) we can have at most\n\\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points before the sum would drop below\n\\(2\\).  Hence the claimed bound holds. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for arbitrary \\(k\\) when \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge4\\) and an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n**Sunny lines.**  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = x + i .\n\\]\n\nThe slope of every \\(S_i\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\nBecause the constants \\(i\\) are distinct, the lines \\(S_i\\) are distinct.\nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on some \\(S_i\\) exactly when \\(b-a=i\\).\n\n**Non‑sunny lines.**  \nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-k\\) let  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j .\n\\]\n\nThese are horizontal, hence non‑sunny, and are distinct.\n\n**Coverage proof.**  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the sunny line\n\\(S_{\\,b-a}\\).\n\n*Otherwise* \\(b-a\\ge k\\).  Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\nb = (a+b)-a \\le (n+1)-a .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) then \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k\\), whence\n\\(b-a\\ge (n-k+1)-k = n-2k+1\\ge 1\\).  In this situation \\(b\\le n-k\\) cannot\nhold, so the previous inequality forces \\(b\\le n-k\\).  Consequently\n\\(b\\) is one of the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\) and the point lies on the\nhorizontal line \\(H_{\\,b}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is contained in at least one of the constructed\nlines.  The total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nk\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-k)\\;(\\text{horizontal}) = n ,\n\\]\n\nand all of them are distinct.  Hence for the given \\(k\\) a suitable configuration\nexists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Completion of the classification\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the exhaustive analysis of Section 1 yields the admissible\n  values \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\n* For every \\(n\\ge4\\) the construction of Section 3 works for **every**\n  integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\nTherefore the set of all pairs \\((n,k)\\) that satisfy the conditions of the\nproblem is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\} &\\text{if } n=3,\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\} &\\text{if } n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**The problem is completely solved.**"
