            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\);\notherwise it is **non‑sunny** (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Maximum number of points of \\(T_n\\) on a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line that contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\).\nWrite the reduced slope of \\(L\\) as \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) with\n\\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\notin\\{0,\\pm1,\\infty\\}\\).\nIf \\((x,y)\\in L\\cap T_n\\) then the next lattice point of \\(L\\) is\n\\((x+q,\\;y+p)\\); consequently the sum \\(x+y\\) increases by \\(|p|+|q|\\ge 2\\).\nSince the smallest possible sum of a point of \\(T_n\\) is \\(2\\) and the\nlargest is \\(n+1\\), the number of lattice points of \\(T_n\\) that can lie on\n\\(L\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)-2}{|p|+|q|}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\le 1+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus every sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### The case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).\n\nAll lines determined by two points of \\(T_3\\) are listed below together with\ntheir slopes.\n\n| two points | equation | slope |\n|------------|----------|-------|\n| \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) | \\(y=x\\) | \\(1\\) (sunny) |\n| \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) | \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\) | \\(-\\tfrac12\\) (sunny) |\n| \\((2,1),(3,2)\\) | \\(y=2x-3\\) | \\(2\\) (sunny) |\n| any pair sharing a common \\(x\\)– or \\(y\\)–coordinate, or lying on a line\n  \\(x+y=c\\) | – | non‑sunny |\n\nHence the only sunny lines that meet \\(T_3\\) are the three displayed above;\neach of them contains exactly two points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n*Construction for \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the three horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\;y=2,\\;y=3\\).  All are non‑sunny and\ntogether contain the six points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n*Construction for \\(k=1\\).*  \nChoose the sunny line \\(y=x\\) (covers \\((1,1),(2,2)\\)) together with the two\nnon‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) (covers \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\)) and \\(y=1\\) (covers\n\\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\)).  Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n*Construction for \\(k=3\\).*  \nTake the three sunny lines listed in the table; they are distinct and together\ncontain all six points.\n\n*Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).*  \nAssume that exactly two of the three lines are sunny.  Because each sunny line\ncontains precisely the two points shown in the table, the two sunny lines can\ncover at most four distinct points of \\(T_3\\).  The two points that remain\nuncovered must be a pair of the form \\(\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\}\\), \\(\\{(2,1),(1,3)\\}\\) or\n\\(\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\}\\).  In each case the unique line through the uncovered pair\nis one of the three sunny lines already used, contradicting the requirement\nthat the third line be non‑sunny.  Hence no configuration with exactly two\nsunny lines exists.\n\nConsequently for \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Covering the “upper” part of the triangle by sunny lines)\n\nFor any integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nU_m=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b\\ge n-m+2\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(U_m\\) consists of the \\(m\\) rows of the triangle that are farthest from the\norigin.  The following holds.\n\n\\[\n\\text{The set }U_m\\text{ can be partitioned into exactly }m\\text{ distinct sunny lines.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n-m+2),\\qquad Q_i=(m+1,\\;n-i+1).\n\\]\n\nBoth points belong to \\(U_m\\) because  \n\n\\[\ni+(n-m+2)=n-m+2+i\\ge n-m+2,\\qquad\n(m+1)+(n-i+1)=n-m+2+(m-i+1)\\ge n-m+2,\n\\]\nand each sum does not exceed \\(n+1\\).\n\nLet \\(L_i\\) be the line through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\).  Its slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-i+1)-(n-m+2)}{(m+1)-i}\n=\\frac{m-i}{m+1-i}=1-\\frac{1}{\\,m+1-i\\,}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m+1-i\\ge 1\\), the slope is never \\(0\\), never infinite, and never\n\\(-1\\); therefore every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\nThe two points \\(P_i,Q_i\\) lie on no other line \\(L_j\\) (\\(j\\neq i\\)):\nif \\(j<i\\) then the \\(x\\)-coordinate of \\(P_i\\) equals \\(i>j\\), while the\n\\(x\\)-coordinates of all points of \\(L_j\\) are \\(\\le j\\); similarly for\n\\(j>i\\).  Hence the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\) are pairwise disjoint.\n\nFinally, every point of \\(U_m\\) belongs to some \\(L_i\\).  Indeed, a point\n\\((a,b)\\in U_m\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\ge n-m+2\\).  Write \\(c=n-m+2\\).  If\n\\(a\\le m\\) then \\(b\\ge c\\) and the point coincides with \\(P_a\\); if\n\\(a\\ge m+1\\) then \\(b\\le n-a+1\\) and the point coincides with \\(Q_{n-a+1}\\).\nThus the collection \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{m}\\) covers all of \\(U_m\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for arbitrary \\(k\\) when \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 4\\) and let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\nPut \\(t=n-k\\).\n\n1. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,t\\) take the diagonal line  \n\n   \\[\n   D_j:\\;x+y=j+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(D_j\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and therefore is non‑sunny.  \n   The union \\(\\bigcup_{j=1}^{t}D_j\\) consists exactly of the points of\n   \\(T_n\\) whose sum \\(a+b\\) is at most \\(t+1\\).\n\n2. **Sunny lines.**  \n   The points that are not covered by the diagonals are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   U_k=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b\\ge n-k+2\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the “upper” part of the triangle of size \\(k\\).  \n   By Lemma 2 the set \\(U_k\\) can be partitioned into \\(k\\) distinct sunny\n   lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n3. **Verification.**  \n   The \\(t\\) diagonal lines together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines give a total\n   of \\(t+k=n\\) distinct lines.  Every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs either to a\n   diagonal (if its sum is \\(\\le t+1\\)) or to one of the lines \\(L_i\\) (if its\n   sum is \\(\\ge n-k+2\\)).  Hence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly\n   \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two requirements exists when \\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the classification\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the exhaustive analysis of the first part shows that the only\n  possible values are \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge 4\\) the construction above works for **all**\n  integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible pairs \\((n,k)\\) is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justificat