1}\n   \\]\n\n   Condition (1) forces the elements of \\(S\\) to be the *smallest* \\(t\\)\n   sums, namely  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{2,3,\\dots ,t+1\\}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, if a larger sum were omitted, its class would contain more\n   than \\(t\\) points, contradicting (1).\n\n5. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   From (2) the largest omitted sum is \\(t+1\\); its class contains\n   \\(t\\) points.  To cover those \\(t\\) points we need at least \\(t\\)\n   distinct non\u2011anti\u2011diagonal lines, i.e. we need  \n\n   \\[\n   t\\le k . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (3) with the definition \\(t=|S|\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   |S|\\le k . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   On the other hand, by construction we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines,\n   so the total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   n = (\\text{anti\u2011diagonals}) + (\\text{other lines})\n     = (n-|S|) + k .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   |S| = k . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting (5) into (3) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k \\le k \\quad\\text{and}\\quad k = |S| \\le k .\n   \\]\n\n   The only way (5) can hold together with (3) is when  \n\n   \\[\n   k = |S| \\le 3 .\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore **no configuration can contain four or more sunny lines**.  \n\n6. **Impossibility of exactly two sunny lines.**  \n   If \\(k=2\\) then \\(|S|=2\\) by (5).  \n   Consequently the omitted sums are \\(\\{2,3\\}\\); their point\u2011sets are  \n\n   \\[\n   D_2\\cap T_n=\\{(1,1)\\},\\qquad\n   D_3\\cap T_n=\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The two sunny lines can each meet a given sum\u2011class in at most one\n   point (Lemma\u202f1), so together they can cover at most two points of\n   \\(D_3\\).  Hence one of \\((1,2)\\) or \\((2,1)\\) would remain uncovered,\n   a contradiction.  Thus **\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).**\n\n7. **Existence for the remaining values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take all anti\u2011diagonals \\(D_s\\;(2\\le s\\le n+1)\\).  \n   They are non\u2011sunny,