 \n   Any line that contains \\((1,2)\\) and is not the anti\u2011diagonal \\(x+y=3\\) has\n   slope different from \\(-1\\); the same holds for a line through \\((2,1)\\).\n   Because the two points have different \\(x\\)\u2011coordinates, the two required\n   lines cannot be parallel to the \\(y\\)\u2011axis, and because they have different\n   \\(y\\)\u2011coordinates they cannot be parallel to the \\(x\\)\u2011axis.  \n   Hence the only way to cover both points with two distinct non\u2011anti\u2011diagonal\n   lines is to use the two anti\u2011diagonals \\(D_2\\) and \\(D_3\\).  But \\(D_2\\) is the\n   line \\(x+y=2\\) (which contains only the point \\((1,1)\\)) and is **not**\n   allowed to be omitted, contradicting the description of \\(S\\) in (3).  \n   Therefore a configuration with \\(k=2\\) cannot exist.\n\n8. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 4\\).**  \n\n   Suppose \\(k\\ge 4\\).  By Condition\u202fC the \\(k\\) sunny lines must contain the\n   \\(k\\) points listed in (4).  Consider the two extreme points\n   \\((1,k)\\) and \\((k,1)\\).  Any line through \\((1,k)\\) has equation\n   \\[\n   y = m(x-1)+k \\qquad (m\\neq 0,\\infty,-1).\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(x=k\\) gives the \\(y\\)\u2013coordinate of the intersection with the\n   vertical line \\(x=k\\):\n   \\[\n   y = m(k-1)+k .\n   \\]\n   For this line to pass through the other forced point \\((k,1)\\) we would need\n   \\(m(k-1)+k = 1\\), i.e. \\(m = \\dfrac{1-k}{k-1} = -1\\), which is forbidden for a\n   sunny line.  Consequently **no single sunny line can contain both\n   \\((1,k)\\) and \\((k,1)\\)**.  Because the \\(k\\) sunny lines are distinct,\n   each of them can contain at most one of the two extreme points, so at least\n   two different sunny lines are required for these two points.\n\n   Now look at the three points \\((1,k),\\,(2,k-1),\\,(3,k-2)\\).  Any line that\n   contains two of them has slope\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(k-1)-k}{2-1}=-1,\\qquad\n   \\frac{(k-2)-k}{3-1}=-\\frac{2}{2}=-1,\n