\\\\n   S_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12(x-1)+(n-1),\\\\\n   S_3 &: y =  (x-1)+(n-2).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   The six points of \\(T_n\\) that are not on any of the horizontals are  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,n),\\;(1,n-1),\\;(2,n-2),\\;(1,n-2),\\;(2,n-1),\\;(3,n-2),\n   \\]\n\n   and a direct substitution shows that each of them lies on exactly one of\n   \\(S_1,S_2,S_3\\).  All three slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so\n   the three lines are sunny and distinct from the horizontals.\n\n10. **Conclusion.**  \n    The arguments above prove that a configuration exists precisely for\n    \\(k=0,1,3\\) and for no other non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\).  Hence the set of\n    admissible values is  \n\n    \\[\n    \\boxed{\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}} .\n    \\]"
