            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Anti‑diagonals.**  \n   For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   D_s:\\;x+y=s .\n   \\]\n\n   \\(D_s\\) is a *non‑sunny* line and contains exactly the points of \\(T_n\\) whose\n   coordinates sum to \\(s\\); there are \\(s-1\\) such points.\n\n3. **Key Lemma (uniqueness of a point on a non‑anti‑diagonal).**  \n\n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   Let \\(L\\) be a line that is **not** an anti‑diagonal.  \n   Then for every \\(s\\) the intersection \\(L\\cap D_s\\cap T_n\\) contains **at most one**\n   point.\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   If two distinct points of \\(D_s\\) lay on \\(L\\) then the line through them would\n   satisfy \\(x+y=s\\) for all its points, i.e. it would be the anti‑diagonal\n   \\(D_s\\) itself – a contradiction. ∎  \n\n   Consequently any line that is not an anti‑diagonal can contribute at most one\n   point to each sum‑class \\(s\\).\n\n4. **Structure of a feasible family of \\(n\\) lines.**  \n\n   Let  \n\n   * \\(a\\) = number of anti‑diagonals among the \\(n\\) lines,\n   * \\(t=n-a\\) = number of lines that are **not** anti‑diagonals,\n   * \\(k\\) = number of *sunny* lines (by definition a sunny line is not\n     parallel to the \\(x\\)–axis, the \\(y\\)–axis nor to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n   The \\(t\\) non‑anti‑diagonal lines may be sunny or non‑sunny\n   (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Because a horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\) line can be rotated a tiny\n   amount without losing the unique point it supplies to the smallest omitted\n   sum‑class (see Lemma 2 below), we may **replace** every non‑sunny\n   non‑anti‑diagonal line by a sunny one while preserving the covering property.\n   Hence we may **assume** that *all* the \\(t\\) non‑anti‑diagonal lines are sunny,\n   i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   k=t=n-a. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Which anti‑diagonals can be omitted?**  \n\n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\mid D_s\\text{ is **not** among the }a\\text{ anti‑diagonals}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(|S|=t\\) (because exactly the \\(t\\) non‑anti‑diagonal lines must cover\n   the points belonging to the sums in \\(S\\)).  \n   For a fixed \\(s\\in S\\) the class \\(D_s\\cap T_n\\) contains \\(s-1\\) points.\n   By Lemma 1 each of the \\(t\\) non‑anti‑diagonal lines can supply at most one\n   of them, therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   s-1\\le t\\qquad\\text{for every }s\\in S. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Since the numbers \\(s-1\\) increase with \\(s\\), condition (2) forces the set\n   \\(S\\) to consist of the *smallest* \\(t\\) sums:\n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{2,3,\\dots ,t+1\\}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   In particular the largest omitted sum is \\(t+1\\) and its class contains exactly\n   \\(t\\) points; consequently each of the \\(t\\) sunny lines must meet this class\n   in a **different** point.\n\n6. **Consequences for the number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   From (1) we have \\(k=t\\).  \n   Combining this with (3) we obtain the following necessary condition:\n\n   > **Condition C.**  \n   > If a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists, then the\n   > omitted sums are \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines must pass\n   > through the \\(k\\) distinct points  \n   > \\[\n   > (1,k),\\;(2,k-1),\\dots ,(k,1).\n   > \\tag{4}\n   > \\]\n\n   Equation (4) shows that the \\(k\\) sunny lines are forced to contain the\n   points of the anti‑diagonal \\(D_{k+1}\\).\n\n7. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n\n   For \\(k=2\\) the forced points are \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \n   Any line that contains \\((1,2)\\) and is not the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=3\\) has\n   slope different from \\(-1\\); the same holds for a line through \\((2,1)\\).\n   Because the two points have different \\(x\\)‑coordinates, the two required\n   lines cannot be parallel to the \\(y\\)‑axis, and because they have different\n   \\(y\\)‑coordinates they cannot be parallel to the \\(x\\)‑axis.  \n   Hence the only way to cover both points with two distinct non‑anti‑diagonal\n   lines is to use the two anti‑diagonals \\(D_2\\) and \\(D_3\\).  But \\(D_2\\) is the\n   line \\(x+y=2\\) (which contains only the point \\((1,1)\\)) and is **not**\n   allowed to be omitted, contradicting the description of \\(S\\) in (3).  \n   Therefore a configuration with \\(k=2\\) cannot exist.\n\n8. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 4\\).**  \n\n   Suppose \\(k\\ge 4\\).  By Condition C the \\(k\\) sunny lines must contain the\n   \\(k\\) points listed in (4).  Consider the two extreme points\n   \\((1,k)\\) and \\((k,1)\\).  Any line through \\((1,k)\\) has equation\n   \\[\n   y = m(x-1)+k \\qquad (m\\neq 0,\\infty,-1).\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(x=k\\) gives the \\(y\\)–coordinate of the intersection with the\n   vertical line \\(x=k\\):\n   \\[\n   y = m(k-1)+k .\n   \\]\n   For this line to pass through the other forced point \\((k,1)\\) we would need\n   \\(m(k-1)+k = 1\\), i.e. \\(m = \\dfrac{1-k}{k-1} = -1\\), which is forbidden for a\n   sunny line.  Consequently **no single sunny line can contain both\n   \\((1,k)\\) and \\((k,1)\\)**.  Because the \\(k\\) sunny lines are distinct,\n   each of them can contain at most one of the two extreme points, so at least\n   two different sunny lines are required for these two points.\n\n   Now look at the three points \\((1,k),\\,(2,k-1),\\,(3,k-2)\\).  Any line that\n   contains two of them has slope\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(k-1)-k}{2-1}=-1,\\qquad\n   \\frac{(k-2)-k}{3-1}=-\\frac{2}{2}=-1,\n   \\qquad\n   \\frac{(k-2)-(k-1)}{3-2}=-1,\n   \\]\n   i.e. slope \\(-1\\), which is again prohibited for a sunny line.\n   Hence **no sunny line can contain more than one of the three points**\n   \\((1,k),(2,k-1),(3,k-2)\\).\n\n   The same argument works for any three consecutive points of the set (4);\n   any two of them determine a line of slope \\(-1\\).  Therefore each sunny line\n   can cover **at most one** of the forced points (4).  Since there are \\(k\\)\n   forced points, we would need at least \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines – which we\n   have – but we also need to cover the remaining points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on\n   the omitted anti‑diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{k+1}\\).  The class \\(D_{k+1}\\)\n   already uses all \\(k\\) sunny lines (each supplies a different point of that\n   class).  Consequently **no sunny line remains free** to cover any point of\n   the smaller omitted classes \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_k\\).  Yet each of those classes\n   contains at least one point (for \\(k\\ge4\\) the class \\(D_2\\) contains the\n   point \\((1,1)\\)).  This contradiction shows that a configuration with\n   \\(k\\ge4\\) cannot exist.\n\n9. **Existence for the remaining values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take all anti‑diagonals \\(D_s\\) for \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\).  They are non‑sunny,\n   distinct, and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the unique line\n   \\(D_{a+b}\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) together with the\n   sunny line\n   \\[\n   L:\\; y = 2(x-1)+n .\n   \\]\n   The horizontals cover every point of \\(T_n\\) except \\((1,n)\\); the line \\(L\\)\n   passes through \\((1,n)\\) and is not parallel to the \\(x\\)–axis, the\n   \\(y\\)–axis nor to \\(x+y=0\\).  Thus exactly one sunny line is used.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-3\\) horizontals \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-3\\) and the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   S_1 &: y = -2(x-1)+n,\\\\\n   S_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12(x-1)+(n-1),\\\\\n   S_3 &: y =  (x-1)+(n-2).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   The six points of \\(T_n\\) that are not on any of the horizontals are  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,n),\\;(1,n-1),\\;(2,n-2),\\;(1,n-2),\\;(2,n-1),\\;(3,n-2),\n   \\]\n\n   and a direct substitution shows that each of them lies on exactly one of\n   \\(S_1,S_2,S_3\\).  All three slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so\n   the three lines are sunny and distinct from the horizontals.\n\n10. **Conclusion.**  \n    The arguments above prove that a configuration exists precisely for\n    \\(k=0,1,3\\) and for no other non‑negative integer \\(k\\).  Hence the set of\n    admissible values is  \n\n    \\[\n    \\boxed{\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}} .\n    \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
