ition a sunny line is not parallel\n     to the \\(x\\)–axis, the \\(y\\)–axis nor to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n   By a tiny rotation argument (any non‑sunny non‑anti‑diagonal line can be\n   rotated a little without destroying the unique point it supplies to each\n   required sum‑class), we may replace every non‑sunny non‑anti‑diagonal line by\n   a sunny one while preserving the covering property.  Hence we may **assume**\n   that all \\(t\\) non‑anti‑diagonal lines are sunny, i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   k=t=n-a. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Which anti‑diagonals may be omitted?**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\mid D_s\\text{ is **not** among the }a\\text{ anti‑diagonals}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Then \\(|S|=t\\) (the \\(t\\) sunny lines must cover the points belonging to the\n   sums in \\(S\\)).  For a fixed \\(s\\in S\\) the class \\(D_s\\cap T_n\\) contains\n   \\(s-1\\) points, and by Lemma 1 each sunny line can supply at most one of them;\n   therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   s-1\\le t\\qquad\\text{for every }s\\in S. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Since the numbers \\(s-1\\) increase with \\(s\\), (2) forces \\(S\\) to be the set\n   of the smallest \\(t\\) sums:\n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{2,3,\\dots ,t+1\\}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   In particular the largest omitted sum is \\(t+1\\) and its class contains\n   exactly \\(t\\) points; consequently each of the \\(t\\) sunny lines must meet\n   this class in a **different** point.\n\n6. **Forced points.**  \n   From (1) we have \\(k=t\\).  Combining (3) with Lemma 1 we obtain the following\n   necessary condition:\n\n   > **Condition C.**  \n   > If a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists, then the\n   > omitted sums are \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines must pass\n   > through the \\(k\\) distinct points  \n   > \\[\n   > (1,k),\\;(2,k-1),\\dots ,(k,1). \\tag{4}\n   > \\]\n\n   Equation (4) shows that the sunny lines are forced to contain the points of\n   the anti‑diagonal \\(D_{k+1}\\).\n\n7. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   For \\(k=2\\) the forced points are \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  Any line through\n   \\((1,2)\\) that is not the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=3\\) has slope different from\n   \\(-1\\); the same holds for a line through \\((2,1)\\).  Because the two points\n   have different \\(x\\)‑coordinates, the two required lines cannot be parallel\n   to the \\(y\\)‑axis, and because they have different \\(y\\)‑coordinates they\n   cannot be parallel to the \\(x\\)‑axis.  Hence the only way to cover both\n   points with two distinct non‑anti‑diagonal lines would be to use the two\n   anti‑diagonals \\(D_2\\) and \\(D_3\\).  But \\(D_2\\) (the line \\(x+y=2\\))\n   contains only the point \\((1,1)\\) and must be present among the chosen\n   anti‑diagonals, contradicting (3).  Therefore a configuration with\n   \\(k=2\\) cannot exist.\n\n8. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 4\\).**  \n   Assume \\(k\\ge 4\\).  By Condition C the \\(k\\) sunny lines must contain the\n   points listed in (4).  Any two *consecutive* points of (4) lie on a line of\n   slope \\(-1\\); for example the line through \\((i,k+1-i)\\) and\n   \\((i+1,k-i)\\) satisfies\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(k-i)-(k+1-i)}{(i+1)-i}=-1 .\n   \\]\n   Because a sunny line is prohibited from having slope \\(-1\\), **no sunny\n   line can contain more than one of the points (4)**.  Hence each of the \\(k\\)\n   sunny lines can cover at most one forced point, and all \\(k\\) forced points\n   are already used.\n\n   The class \\(D_{k+1}\\) (the set of forced points) therefore consumes all\n   sunny lines; none remains to cover any point of the smaller omitted classes\n   \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_k\\).  Yet each of those classes contains at least one point\n   (for instance \\((1,1)\\in D_2\\)).  Consequently the required covering is\n   impossible for any \\(k\\ge 4\\).\n\n9. **Existence constructions.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take **all** anti‑diagonals\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_0=\\{D_s\\mid 2\\le s\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\