& a=1,2,3\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the six points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,n),\\;(1,n-1),\\;(2,n-1),\\;(1,n-2),\\;(2,n-2),\\;(3,n-2).\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification that each of these six points lies on one of \\(S_1,S_2,S_3\\).*\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n   \\text{Point }(a,b) & \\text{Line equation} & \\text{Check} \\\\ \\hline\n   (1,n)      & S_1:\\; y=-2(x-1)+n & -2(1-1)+n=n \\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (1,n-1)    & S_2:\\; y=-\\tfrac12(x-1)+(n-1) & -\\tfrac12(0)+(n-1)=n-1 \\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (2,n-1)    & S_1:\\; y=-2(x-1)+n & -2(2-1)+n=n-2\\neq n-1\\;\\; \\text{but}\\\\\n               & S_3:\\; y= (x-1)+(n-2) & (2-1)+(n-2)=n-1 \\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (1,n-2)    & S_3:\\; y= (x-1)+(n-2) & (1-1)+(n-2)=n-2 \\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (2,n-2)    & S_2:\\; y=-\\tfrac12(x-1)+(n-1) & -\\tfrac12(1)+(n-1)=n-\\tfrac32\\neq n-2\\\\\n               & S_3:\\; y= (x-1)+(n-2) & (2-1)+(n-2)=n-1\\neq n-2\\\\\n               & S_1:\\; y=-2(x-1)+n & -2(1)+n=n-2 \\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (3,n-2)    & S_2:\\; y=-\\tfrac12(x-1)+(n-1) & -\\tfrac12(2)+(n-1)=n-2 \\;\\checkmark\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   Every listed point satisfies the equation of the indicated line, so all six\n   are covered.  All other points of \\(T_n\\) have \\(b\\le n-3\\) and are covered\n   by the appropriate horizontal \\(H_b\\).\n\n   Hence the family consisting of the \\(n-3\\) horizontals and the three sunny\n   lines \\(S_1,S_2,S_3\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\) and uses exactly three\n   sunny lines.\n\n   *Special case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   When \\(n=3\\) the set of horizontals is empty (\\(n-3=0\\)); the three sunny\n   lines above become\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   S_1 &: y=-2x+5,\\\\\n   S_2 &: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\\\\n   S_3 &: y= x-1,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   and the six points of \\(T_3\\) are precisely the six points verified in the\n   table, so the construction works for the smallest admissible \\(n\\).\n\n10. **Conclusion.**  \n    The necessary condition derived from