f vertical lines (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)),  \n   * \\(s\\) = number of sunny lines (neither horizontal, vertical nor an\n     anti‑diagonal).\n\n   Then  \n\n   \\[\n   n=a+h+v+s . \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n   Every non‑anti‑diagonal line meets each omitted sum‑class in at most one\n   point (Lemma 1).  In a feasible configuration each omitted sum‑class must be\n   completely covered, so the **only** way to achieve this is to have exactly\n   one non‑anti‑diagonal line for each omitted class.  Hence the number of\n   omitted classes equals the total number of non‑anti‑diagonal lines:\n\n   \\[\n   t:=h+v+s=n-a . \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover, each omitted class contains at most \\(t\\) points; therefore the\n   omitted classes are precisely the smallest \\(t\\) sums:\n\n   \\[\n   S_{\\text{omit}}=\\{2,3,\\dots ,t+1\\}. \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Eliminating horizontal and vertical lines.**  \n   Suppose a horizontal (or vertical) line \\(H\\) occurs.  By (4.3) the omitted\n   class that contains the point of \\(H\\) is \\(D_{t+1}\\); this class consists of\n   the \\(t\\) points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,t),\\;(2,t-1),\\dots ,(t,1). \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n\n   Each of these points has a distinct \\(y\\)‑coordinate, so a horizontal line\n   can contain **at most one** of them; similarly a vertical line can contain\n   at most one.  Rotating a horizontal (or vertical) line a tiny amount about\n   the unique forced point it passes through yields a line with a slope different\n   from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); the rotation does not affect any other required point\n   because the line previously covered no other point of \\(T_n\\) (Lemma 1).  Thus\n   any feasible family can be transformed, without changing the set of covered\n   points, into a family in which **all** non‑anti‑diagonal lines are sunny.\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   s=t=n-a . \\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Forced points (Condition C).**  \n   From (4.3) and (5.2) we obtain the necessary condition:\n\n   > **Condition C.**  \n   > If a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists, then  \n   > \\[\n   > k=n-a,\\qquad \n   > S_{\\text{omit}}=\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\},\n   > \\]  \n   > and the \\(k\\) sunny lines must pass through the \\(k\\) distinct points  \n   > \\[\n   > (1,k),\\;(2,k-1),\\dots ,(k,1). \\tag{6.1}\n   > \\]\n\n   The points in (6.1) lie on the anti‑diagonal \\(D_{k+1}\\).\n\n7. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   For \\(k=2\\) the forced points are \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \n   The omitted sum‑class \\(D_{3}\\) also contains the point \\((1,2)\\) and\n   \\((2,1)\\); the remaining omitted class is \\(D_{2}\\) which contains only\n   \\((1,1)\\).  Because we have exactly two sunny lines, each must contain one of\n   the forced points (Condition C).  A line through \\((1,2)\\) that is not the\n   anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=3\\) cannot pass through \\((1,1)\\) (different \\(y\\)‑value);\n   similarly a line through \\((2,1)\\) cannot pass through \\((1,1)\\).  Hence the\n   point \\((1,1)\\) would remain uncovered, contradicting the covering\n   requirement.  Therefore **no configuration with \\(k=2\\) exists**.\n\n8. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 4\\).**  \n   Assume \\(k\\ge 4\\).  By Condition C the \\(k\\) sunny lines must contain the\n   points listed in (6.1).  Any two consecutive points of (6.1) lie on a line of\n   slope \\(-1\\); for example the line through \\((i,k+1-i)\\) and\n   \\((i+1,k-i)\\) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(k-i)-(k+1-i)}{(i+1)-i}=-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since a sunny line is prohibited from having slope \\(-1\\), **no sunny line\n   can contain more than one forced point**.  Thus each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines\n   is forced to cover exactly one of the points (6.1), and all sunny lines are\n   already used up covering the anti‑diagonal \\(D_{k+1}\\).  The omitted classes\n   \\(D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{k}\\) each contain at least one point (e.g. \\((1,1)\\in\n   D_{2}\\)), but there is no remaining line to cover them.  Hence a configuration\n   with \\(k\\ge 4\\) is impossible.\n\n9. **Existence constructions.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take **all** anti‑diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{0}=\\{D_s\\mid 2\\le s\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and set \\(s=a+b\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).  Substituting\n   \\((a,b)\\) into the equation \\(x+y=s\\) of \\(D_s\\) gives \\(a+b=s\\), which is\n   true; therefore \\((a,b)\\in D_s\\).  Every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one\n   line of \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\).  No line is sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_i:\\;y=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n-1)\n   \\]\n\n   together with the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y=2(x-1)+n\\qquad\\bigl(\\text{slope }2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   - If \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(b\\le n-1\\), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the\n     horizontal line \\(H_b\\).  \n   - The only point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(b=n\\) is \\((1,n)\\) (because \\(a\\ge1\\) and\n     \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  Substituting \\((1,n)\\) into the equation of \\(L\\) gives\n     \\(n=2(1-1)+n\\), which holds; thus \\((1,n)\\in L\\).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, exactly one line (\\(L\\)) is sunny,\n   and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   H_i &: y=i \\qquad (1\\le i\\le n-3),\\\\[2pt]\n   S_1 &: y = -2(x-1)+n,\\\\\n   S_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12(x-1)+(n-1),\\\\\n   S_3 &: y =  \\;\\;(x-1)+(n-2).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   The slopes \\(-2,-\\tfrac12,1\\) are none of \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore\n   \\(S_1,S_2,S_3\\) are sunny and distinct from the horizontals.\n\n   *Points not covered by horizontals.*  \n   A point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) fails to lie on a horizontal line exactly when\n   \\(b\\ge n-2\\).  The admissible pairs are\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   b & \\text{possible }a \\\\ \\hline\n   n   & a=1\\\\\n   n-1 & a=1,2\\\\\n   n-2 & a=1,2,3\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the six points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,n),\\;(1,n-1),\\;(2,n-1),\\;(1,n-2),\\;(2,n-2),\\;(3,n-2).\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification that each of these six points lies on one of \\(S_1,S_2,S_3\\).*\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n   \\text{Point }(a,b) & \\text{Line equation} & \\text{Check} \\\\ \\hline\n   (1,n)      & S_1:\\; y=-2(x-1)+n & -2(0)+n=n \\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (1,n-1)    & S_2:\\; y=-\\tfrac12(x-1)+(n-1) & -\\tfrac12(0)+(n-1)=n-1 \\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (2,n-1)    & S_3:\\; y= (x-1)+(n-2) & (2-1)+(n-2)=n-1 \\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (1,n-2)    & S_3:\\; y= (x-1)+(n-2) & (1-1)+(n-2)=n-2 \\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (2,n-2)    & S_1:\\; y=-2(x-1)+n & -2(1)+n=n-2 \\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (3,n-2)    & S_2:\\; y=-\\tfrac12(x-1)+(n-1) & -\\tfrac12(2)+(n-1)=n-2 \\;\\checkmark\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   Every point with \\(b\\ge n-2\\) is thus covered.  All remaining points of\n   \\(T_n\\) have \\(b\\le n-3\\) and are covered by the appropriate horizontal line\n   \\(H_b\\).  Consequently the family consisting of the \\(n-3\\) horizontals and\n   the three sunny lines \\(S_1,S_2,S_3\\) covers all of \\(T_n\\) and uses exactly\n   three sunny lines.\n\n   *Special case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   When \\(n=3\\) the set of horizontals is empty; the three sunny lines become  \n\n   \\[\n   y=-2x+5,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=x-1,\n   \\]\n\n   and the verification table above reduces to the six points of \\(T_3\\), so the\n   construction works for the smallest admissible \\(n\\).\n\n10. **Conclusion.**  \n    Lemma 1 forces the omitted sum‑classes to be the smallest ones, which in turn\n    forces Condition C.  Condition C eliminates all \\(k\\) except \\(0,1,3\\); the\n    explicit constructions above show that each of these three values is indeed\n    attainable.  Therefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which\n    the required configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists is  \n\n    \\[\